# ─── ENVIRONMENT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reference for every variable the application reads. `.env*` is gitignored and
# this file is the only committed member of that family, so it must never contain
# a real value.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Full guide: docs/ENV_SETUP.md
#
# Variables marked REQUIRED cause the process to refuse to start when absent or
# blank. lib/env.ts throws instead of falling back, because four different
# hardcoded fallback JWT secrets once meant a missing variable left some routes
# signing tokens with a string published in this repository.


# ─── SECRETS (REQUIRED) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Signs candidate and staff session cookies. Any length; 32+ random bytes is the
# intended minimum.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
JWT_SECRET=

# Pepper mixed into the device-key hash before storage, so a database dump does
# not hand over the keys that would let someone forge a device binding. Distinct
# from JWT_SECRET.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
DEVICE_PEPPER=

# Shared secret for the WordPress registration form, sent as `x-api-key` on
# POST /api/register. Unrelated to the in-app form at /register, which posts to
# /api/apply and does not use this value.
#
# Only relevant when WordPress remains a second registration route. Blank leaves
# /api/register failing closed, which is the correct state for a retired CMS form:
# a dead endpoint beats a live one nobody is watching.
WP_TO_APP_SECRET=


# ─── STAFF ACCESS (REQUIRED) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two PINs rather than one. A single shared STAFF_ACCESS_TOKEN let a check-in
# operator reach /admissions and award scholarships, while an admissions officer
# read the attendance dashboard. The split is deliberate: `staff` runs the door,
# `admissions` decides who wins.
#
# requireEnv() throws if either is blank, and the comparison is constant-time.
# Six digits is sufficient — the limit is 5 attempts per IP+device per 15 minutes.
# Neither PIN belongs anywhere else in any system.
STAFF_PIN=
ADMISSIONS_PIN=


# ─── DATABASE (REQUIRED) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# PostgreSQL connection string. Neon serverless works; any Postgres does.
#
# `sslmode=verify-full` is written out explicitly because the `pg` driver
# currently treats `require` and `prefer` as aliases of it, and warns that this
# changes in the next major version. Spelling out the intent avoids a future
# upgrade that either tightens the connection or drops the check.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=verify-full

# Set to "true" for a plaintext local database. Anything else, or unset, means TLS
# to the server.
# DATABASE_SSL=false

# Connection pool ceiling per serverless instance. Serverless means many
# instances and this multiplies. Ten is deliberate — see docs/ENV_SETUP.md.
# DB_POOL_MAX=10

# Log every SQL statement. Development only.
# SQL_LOG=true


# ─── REDIS (REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Without these the application still runs, and locks and rate limits fall back to
# a single-process path. That is correct on one instance and not correct on
# several — see docs/ENV_SETUP.md before scaling past one.

# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=


# ─── THE ROUND ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every figure the README quotes is read from config/rules.ts, which reads these.
# Defaults reflect the most recent round.

# Scholarships available across all courses. The per-course cap is this divided by
# the number of courses in config/course-matrix.ts, rounded down.
# TOTAL_SLOTS=546

# How many candidates reach the theory paper. Ranked by score, then finish time.
# QUALIFIED_POOL_SIZE=910

# Hard stop on attendance. Check-in refuses beyond this.
# VENUE_CAPACITY=3500

# Section lengths, in minutes. Enforced from database timestamps rather than a
# browser timer, so a closed tab does not extend a sitting.
# OBJECTIVE_TTL_MINUTES=30
# THEORY_TTL_MINUTES=45

# Minimum words per theory answer to be gradeable.
# THEORY_MIN_WORDS=50

# Objective pass mark, as a percentage.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ is a separate variable on purpose: the candidate-facing rules page
# has to state the number the server enforces, and a client bundle cannot read a
# server variable. Both must agree, or the rules page quotes a figure that is not
# the one being applied.
# OBJECTIVE_PASS_MARK=80
# NEXT_PUBLIC_OBJECTIVE_PASS_MARK=80

# Cohort year, used in copy and on registrants.
# CURRENT_COHORT=2026

# Ticket code format: prefix + "-" + 8 hex characters. A change here is a new
# format, and previously printed tickets stop validating.
# TICKET_PREFIX=TS26
# TICKET_BODY_LENGTH=8


# ─── IDENTITY (OPTIONAL) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All have working defaults. Setting these describes a specific round while the
# repository stays publishable, which keeps a new cohort a configuration change
# rather than a code change.

# EVENT_NAME=Tech Scholarship Programme
# EVENT_SHORT_NAME=Scholarship Programme
# EVENT_ORGANISATION=Programme Office
# EVENT_TAGLINE=A day of assessments, scholarships and careers.
# EVENT_DATE=
# EVENT_VENUE=
# EVENT_ADDRESS=
# EVENT_CONTACT_EMAIL=support@example.com

# Where the portal is deployed. Used in links and QR payloads.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Social links for the confirmation page. Unset means the icons are omitted rather
# than linking somewhere unconfigured.
# SOCIAL_INSTAGRAM=
# SOCIAL_FACEBOOK=
# SOCIAL_LINKEDIN=
# SOCIAL_X=

# Palette for the confirmation page, the email and the QR code.
# BRAND_INK=#141210
# BRAND_ACCENT=#B45309
# BRAND_ACCENT_SOFT=#FEF3C7
# BRAND_PAPER=#FFFFFF
# BRAND_MUTED=#78716C
# BRAND_LINE=#E7E5E4


# ─── EMAIL (OPTIONAL) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Without either provider, registration still succeeds and the ticket is logged
# rather than sent. That converts a missed ticket into a support queue, so it is
# worth configuring one.
#
# Brevo requires no verified sending domain, which makes it the practical choice
# for a first send; only the sender address needs verifying inside Brevo. Resend
# additionally requires a verified domain before it will deliver to arbitrary
# recipients.
#
# MAIL_PROVIDER pins the provider explicitly. When unset, Brevo is used if
# BREVO_API_KEY is present, otherwise Resend.

# MAIL_PROVIDER=brevo
# BREVO_API_KEY=
# BREVO_FROM_EMAIL=
# BREVO_FROM_NAME=

# RESEND_API_KEY=
# MAIL_FROM=Programme Office <no-reply@example.com>


# ─── WORDPRESS FORM FIELDS (OPTIONAL) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# The CMS's field names, not this application's. A WordPress field can be renamed
# here rather than in code. Gravity Forms keys are accepted as fallbacks.

# WP_API_KEY=
# WP_FORM_BLOB_FIELD=event-registration


# ─── PUBLIC REGISTRATION FORM (REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION) ────────────────────────
# The in-app application form at /register, which posts to /api/apply.
#
# That endpoint is public — anyone on the internet can reach it, which is the
# entire point of it. Unlike /api/register there is therefore no shared secret to
# gate it. Turnstile is what prevents a script filling the admissions queue with
# thousands of synthetic applicants, every one of which becomes a real row that
# then counts toward the ranking and the per-course seat totals.
#
# Both keys come from Cloudflare → Turnstile → Add widget. The site key is public
# by design; the secret must never reach the browser.
#
# Unset in development, the form still works and the server warns loudly. Unset in
# PRODUCTION, /api/apply refuses every request rather than quietly serving an
# unprotected public write endpoint.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=


# ─── DIAGNOSTICS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# error | warn | info | debug
# LOG_LEVEL=info
# DEBUG=false